# Network access

```elixir
Mix.install([{:kettlecorn, "~> 0.2.0"}])
```

## About this notebook

Compare denied network access, raw sockets, and HTTP requests. Read
[Getting started](getting-started.livemd) first.

Requires Elixir 1.20 or later on x86_64 or ARM64 Linux or Apple silicon macOS.

```elixir
{:ok, runtime} = Kettlecorn.Install.runtime()
```

## Compare network grants

Choose a grant for the connections the guest needs.

* `network: true` gives sockets (`:gen_tcp`, `:ssl`, DNS) over Wasmtime's
  `wasi:sockets`. The guest does TLS itself and trusts the host's CA
  certificates, unless `:cacerts` names others.
* `http: true` enables `wasi:http`. Kettlecorn's HTTP sender verifies TLS
  with the bundled Mozilla root certificates. The guest sets
  `Kettlecorn.HTTP.ReqAdapter` as Req's default adapter.

Use `true` to allow any reachable destination, including services on
`localhost`. Use `[allow: rules]` to limit the grant to
[listed destinations](../guides/sandbox.md#network-allowlists). This
notebook starts a local HTTP server on `127.0.0.1`.

```elixir
{:ok, listener} =
  :gen_tcp.listen(0, [:binary, ip: {127, 0, 0, 1}, active: false, reuseaddr: true])

{:ok, port} = :inet.port(listener)
url = "http://127.0.0.1:#{port}/"
```

This host-side loop reads complete request headers and returns a fixed response.
Closing the listener stops it:

```elixir
read_request = fn read_request, socket, received ->
  if :binary.match(received, "\r\n\r\n") != :nomatch do
    :ok
  else
    with {:ok, data} <- :gen_tcp.recv(socket, 0, 5_000),
         do: read_request.(read_request, socket, received <> data)
  end
end

serve = fn serve ->
  case :gen_tcp.accept(listener) do
    {:ok, socket} ->
      with :ok <- read_request.(read_request, socket, "") do
        body = "hello from the host"

        :gen_tcp.send(
          socket,
          "HTTP/1.1 200 OK\r\ncontent-length: #{byte_size(body)}\r\n\r\n" <> body
        )
      end

      :gen_tcp.close(socket)
      serve.(serve)

    {:error, :closed} ->
      :ok
  end
end

spawn(fn -> serve.(serve) end)
```

The same code runs in three VMs: without a grant, with `network: true` and
with `http: true`. It opens a TCP connection, then makes a request with
`Kettlecorn.HTTP` and one with Req:

```elixir
code = """
url = "#{url}"

response = fn
  {:ok, %{status: status, body: body}} -> {status, body}
  {:error, %{__exception__: true, reason: reason}} -> {:error, reason}
  {:error, reason} -> {:error, reason}
end

%{
  "tcp" =>
    with({:ok, socket} <- :gen_tcp.connect({127, 0, 0, 1}, #{port}, [], 2_000),
      do: :gen_tcp.close(socket)
    ),
  "Kettlecorn.HTTP" => response.(Kettlecorn.HTTP.request(:get, url)),
  "Req" => response.(Req.get(url, retry: false))
}
"""
```

Expected results:

| Grant | TCP | `Kettlecorn.HTTP` | Req |
|---|---|---|---|
| None | `:eacces` | `:unsupported` | `:network_disabled` |
| `network: true` | Allowed | `:unsupported` | HTTP 200 |
| `http: true` | `:eacces` | HTTP 200 | HTTP 200 |

The host decodes `:network_disabled` as `{:unknown_atom, "network_disabled"}`
because that atom is not loaded on the host; decoding guest output never creates atoms.

```elixir
ok_response = {200, "hello from the host"}

expected = [
  {[],
   %{
     "tcp" => {:error, :eacces},
     "Kettlecorn.HTTP" => {:error, :unsupported},
     "Req" => {:error, {:unknown_atom, "network_disabled"}}
   }},
  {[network: true],
   %{"tcp" => :ok, "Kettlecorn.HTTP" => {:error, :unsupported}, "Req" => ok_response}},
  {[http: true],
   %{"tcp" => {:error, :eacces}, "Kettlecorn.HTTP" => ok_response, "Req" => ok_response}}
]

for {grant, expected_result} <- expected do
  {:ok, %{"value" => result}} =
    Kettlecorn.run(
      code,
      grant ++ [max_fuel: 100_000_000_000, max_lifetime: 30_000, timeout: 10_000]
    )

  ^expected_result = result
  {grant, result}
end
```

## Limit destinations

Allow the local test server by IP address and port. Use the same rule for
the HTTP and sockets grants. The `:public` rule excludes the loopback
address.

```elixir
destination = "127.0.0.1:#{port}"
http_get = ~s<Kettlecorn.HTTP.request(:get, "#{url}")>

tcp_connect = """
case :gen_tcp.connect({127, 0, 0, 1}, #{port}, [], 2_000) do
  {:ok, socket} -> :gen_tcp.close(socket)
  error -> error
end
"""

limits = [max_fuel: 100_000_000_000, max_lifetime: 30_000, timeout: 10_000]

{:ok, %{"value" => {:ok, %{status: 200}}}} =
  Kettlecorn.run(http_get, [http: [allow: [destination]]] ++ limits)

{:ok, %{"value" => {:error, :http_request_denied}}} =
  Kettlecorn.run(http_get, [http: [allow: [:public]]] ++ limits)

{:ok, %{"value" => :ok}} =
  Kettlecorn.run(tcp_connect, [network: [allow: [destination]]] ++ limits)

{:ok, %{"value" => {:error, :eacces}}} =
  Kettlecorn.run(tcp_connect, [network: [allow: [:public]]] ++ limits)

:gen_tcp.close(listener)
```

